+//! Only deliver where we are allowed to: the addresses a delivery may go to.
+//!
+//! A subscriber is a URL, and a tenant chooses it. A URL is not where a request
+//! goes: the address is decided by whoever answers for that name, at the moment a
+//! connection is made. This service dials URLs its own users control, which is
+//! exactly the shape of a server-side request forgery — and the targets that make
+//! one worth attacking are not on the internet. They are the process's own
+//! administration port, the database next to it, an internal service somebody
+//! forgot, and the metadata endpoint every cloud provider runs at a fixed
+//! link-local address and answers with credentials.
+//!
+//! The client itself is `relay/2.7-outbound-client`'s: what it owes is bounded
+//! time, connection reuse and a redirect policy, and none of that is repeated
+//! here. What this module adds is *where*:
+//!
+//! * **a URL is judged in two places, and they answer different questions.**
+//!   [`check`] is what registration can decide with no name service at all: the
+//!   shape of the authority, and names that can only mean "here". [`pin`] is what
+//!   delivery decides: the addresses a name answers with, checked, and then the
+//!   address that is *connected to*. A registration check that claimed to know
+//!   where a name points would be a claim about an address nobody has resolved;
+//! * **the address that is checked is the address that is used.** [`Resolver`] is a
+//!   port, and [`pin`] answers a [`SocketAddr`] the client dials. The bug this
+//!   prevents is the one every naive check has: resolve to validate, then let the
+//!   client resolve *again* to connect. An attacker answers the first question with
+//!   a public address and the second with theirs, and the window between the two is
+//!   the whole attack (DNS rebinding). One resolution, and it is the one that is
+//!   connected to;
+//! * **a refusal is permanent, not retryable.** A subscriber we are not allowed to
+//!   talk to is not a subscriber that is briefly unhappy: another attempt finds the
+//!   same address and the same rule. It reaches the worker as
+//!   [`crate::delivery::SendError::Permanent`], so the delivery is buried and its
+//!   reason is readable in `dead_letters` instead of being retried until the retry
+//!   budget runs out.
+//!
+//! One rule this module deliberately does **not** restate: which schemes a target
+//! may use. `http` and `https` and nothing else is
+//! [`crate::domain::EndpointUrl`]'s rule (`relay/2.1-project-skeleton`), it holds
+//! for every URL that reaches this file, and a second list here would be a second
+//! place to forget one.
+
+use std::fmt;
+use std::future::Future;
+use std::net::{IpAddr, SocketAddr};
