+/// The names that can only mean this machine, or the network it is on.
+///
+/// A name is not an address, so this says nothing about where it points — that is
+/// [`pin`]'s question, and it is asked later, once. What it says is that the name
+/// itself means "here": `localhost` is the loopback interface by definition (RFC
+/// 6761), `.local` is the mDNS namespace of the local link, and `.internal` is the
+/// namespace a private network hands out. A deployment that does not deliver to its
+/// own networks does not deliver to "here" either, and a URL that says "here" is a
+/// URL no delivery can ever be allowed to use.
+///
+/// # Errors
